### Dummy Coding

As noted in Part C, I will be dummy coding the categorical columns of Gender, FamilyHistory, FHCF, Smoke, Monitor and TransMode. Dummy Coding converts categorical (qualitative) variables into a numerical format so that the model can process can process them. This is done by adding binary columns to the dataframe, each representing a category class: a value of 1 indicates the class applies, and 0 indicates it does not. Once encoded, the original categorical columns are no longer needed and are dropped from the dataframe.

```{r echo=TRUE}
#First I will make everything a factor so that they are recognized by levels()
df$Gender <- as.factor(df$Gender)
df$FamilyHistory <- as.factor(df$FamilyHistory)
df$FHCF <- as.factor(df$FHCF)
df$Smoke <- as.factor(df$Smoke)
df$Monitor <- as.factor(df$Monitor)
df$TransMode <- as.factor(df$TransMode)
# Then, I can confirm the class names for each column with levels()
levels(df$Gender)
levels(df$FamilyHistory)
levels(df$FHCF)
levels(df$Smoke)
levels(df$Monitor)
levels(df$TransMode)
# Then, I will create new columns for each class. We always need one less column than in the original, because 0 is meaningful. 
# I will go alphabetically for setting the 0 reference. For Gender, I will use "Male" as the 1, for FamilyHistory-Monitor, I will use "Yes" as the 1, for TransMode I need 3 columns, and will use Automobile as the reference, with Other, Public_Transportation, and Walking as 1 columns. 
# Initializing all columns to 0 
df$Gender_Male <- 0
df$FamilyHistory_yes <- 0
df$FHCF_yes <- 0
df$Smoke_yes <- 0
df$Monitor_yes <- 0
df$TransMode_Other <- 0
df$TransMode_Public_Transportation <- 0
df$TransMode_Walking <- 0
# Now, I am writing an ifelse script to fill the new columns with 1s if the class applies 
df$Gender_Male <- ifelse(df$Gender== "Male", 1, 0)
df$FamilyHistory_yes <- ifelse(df$FamilyHistory == "yes", 1, 0)
df$FHCF_yes <- ifelse(df$FHCF == "yes", 1, 0)
df$Smoke_yes <- ifelse(df$Smoke == "yes", 1, 0)
df$Monitor_yes <- ifelse(df$Monitor == "yes", 1, 0)
df$TransMode_Other <- ifelse(df$TransMode == "Other", 1, 0)
df$TransMode_Public_Transportation <- ifelse(df$TransMode == "Public_Transportation", 1, 0)
df$TransMode_Walking <- ifelse(df$TransMode == "Walking", 1, 0)
```

To dummy code the columns, each column was first converted to a factor using as.factor(), which allows R to retrieve the class levels via levels(). Inspecting the levels confirmed the classes present in each column and informed the structure. Gender has two classes (Female and Male), FamilyHistory, FHCF, Smoke, and Monitor each have two classes (no and yes), and TransMode has four classes (Automobile, Other, Public_Transportation, and Walking). Following the dummy coding rule of one less column than the number of classes, eight new binary columns were created, one for Gender, one for each of the yes/no variables, and three for TransMode. For each variable, the first class alphabetically was the reference class, meaning it is represented by 0 across all dummy columns. Each new column was initialized to 0 and then populated with a 1 using ifelse() wherever the corresponding class applied. 

Now that all of the relevant columns are dummy coded, I can delete the original columns from the dataframe. 

```{r echo=TRUE}
#Note: I used Claude to figure out a quicker way to do this than just rewriting the dataframe, which is what I have done in the past
df$Gender <- NULL
df$FamilyHistory <- NULL
df$FHCF <- NULL
df$Smoke <- NULL
df$Monitor <- NULL
df$TransMode <- NULL
```

### Label Encoding

As noted in Part C, the ordinal character columns Snacking and Alcohol will be label encoded. Unlike dummy coding, label encoding preserves the inherent order of categories by assigning each a numerical value with an order. Before assigning values, the unique classes in each column will be explored using levels() 

```{r echo=TRUE}
#First I will make everything a factor so that they are recognized by levels()
df$Snacking <- as.factor(df$Snacking)
df$Alcohol <- as.factor(df$Alcohol)
#Now, I am examining the classes 
levels(df$Snacking)
levels(df$Alcohol)
```
In both, the order is no < Sometimes < Frequently < Always (note that Alcohol does not have an Always class, as it had too few responses).

For Snacking, I will assign no = 0, Sometimes = 3, Frequently = 6, and Always = 9
For Alcohol, I will assign no = 0, Sometimes = 3, and Frequently = 6. 

```{r echo=TRUE}
#Assigning the codes
df$Snacking <- ifelse(df$Snacking == "no", 0,
               ifelse(df$Snacking == "Sometimes", 3,
               ifelse(df$Snacking == "Frequently", 6,
               ifelse(df$Snacking == "Always", 9, NA))))

df$Alcohol <- ifelse(df$Alcohol == "no", 0,
              ifelse(df$Alcohol == "Sometimes", 3,
              ifelse(df$Alcohol == "Frequently", 6, NA)))
#Checking my work with is.na
sum(is.na(df$Snacking))
sum(is.na(df$Alcohol))
```

The values were assigned in increments of 3, though the specific interval size is arbitrary. What is meaningful is that the values preserve the ordinal ranking of each category. 

I checked the encoding by checking for NA values, which would indicate any unmatched categories. Both columns returned 0, confirming the encoding was applied cleanly. Unlike dummy coding, there is no need to drop the original columns, as the values were overwritten in place. At this point, all columns in the dataframe are numeric.

### Normalization

In this section, I will normalize the data. This means that all numeric columns will be rescaled to a common range, ensuring that no single variable dominates the model simply due to the magnitude of its values.

I will do this via the z score normalization method. This converts all values into their z scores, or how many standard deviations they are from the mean. I am choosing z score normalization over other methods, such as min-max normalization because because z-score normalization is more robust to outliers. Min-max normalization compresses all values into a fixed range (typically 0 to 1), which means outliers can disproportionately influence the scale and squish the remaining values together. Because there are some outliers especially in the Weight category, I will use z score normalization. 

I only need to normalize Age, Height, Weight, FV, NumMeals, Water, PhysAct, TechUse, Alcohol and Snacking, because the dummy coded columns do not need to be normalized. They do not need to be normalized because they are binary, containing only 0s and 1s. Normalizing them would distort their meaning and offer no benefit. 
I will normalize by writing a z score function and then applying it to each column. To calculate the z score, you subtract the mean from the observation and divide by the standard deviation. 

```{r echo=TRUE}
#First, I am writing the z score function 
z_score <- function(x) {(x-mean(x)) / sd(x)
}
# Now, I am applying it to each column
df$Age <- z_score(df$Age)
df$Height <- z_score(df$Height)
df$Weight <- z_score(df$Weight)
df$FV <- z_score(df$FV)
df$NumMeals <- z_score(df$NumMeals)
df$Water <- z_score(df$Water)
df$PhysAct <- z_score(df$PhysAct)
df$TechUse <- z_score(df$TechUse)
df$Alcohol <- z_score(df$Alcohol)
df$Snacking <- z_score(df$Snacking)
# I am checking my work by checking the sd of a column. It should come out to 1. 
sd(df$Weight)
```
Now, all relevant columns are normalized, and they are on the same scale to train the model equally. 

### Splitting the data into Training and Testing

In this section, I will split the data into training and testing data. I am doing this so that I can train the algorithm based off of my dataset, and validate it with another dataset. I cannot validate it with the same dataset as I trained it with because that would produce artificially high accuracy, because the model would simply be memorizing the data it already saw rather than demonstrating a genuine ability to generalize to new, unseen observations.

In my testing process, I will need to tune the k or the amount of nearest neighbors the model is identifying. To reduce sampling bias from any single split, three different seeds will be used to generate three independent 80/20 training and testing splits. Each value of k will be tested across all three splits, and the final prediction will be determined by majority vote across the three models.

To do this, I will first separate the features from the target variable. The feature sets for both training and testing will exclude the target column ObesityLevel, while the target labels will be stored separately and passed to the model during training.

```{r echo=TRUE}
#Separating features and labels
#Features 
df_features <- df[, -which(names(df) == "ObesityLevel")]
df_labels <- df$ObesityLevel


#Seed 1
set.seed(1)
#Selecting 80% of the rows and storing them in sample_index1
sample_index1 <- sample(1:nrow(df_features), 0.8 * nrow(df_features))
train1_features <- df_features[sample_index1, ] #subsetting the 80% of features
test1_features <- df_features[-sample_index1, ] #subsetting the 20% of features 
train1_labels <- df_labels[sample_index1] #attaching the labels that go with the 80%
test1_labels <- df_labels[-sample_index1] #attaching the labels that go with the 20%

#Now, I'm doing the same thing for seed 2
#Seed 2
set.seed(2)
#Selecting 80% of the rows and storing them in sample_index2
sample_index2 <- sample(1:nrow(df_features), 0.8 * nrow(df_features))
train2_features <- df_features[sample_index2, ] #subsetting the 80% of features
test2_features <- df_features[-sample_index2, ] #subsetting the 20% of features 
train2_labels <- df_labels[sample_index2] #attaching the labels that go with the 80%
test2_labels <- df_labels[-sample_index2] #attaching the labels that go with the 20%

#Now, I'm doing the same thing for seed 3
#Seed 3
set.seed(3)
#Selecting 80% of the rows and storing them in sample_index3
sample_index3 <- sample(1:nrow(df_features), 0.8 * nrow(df_features))
train3_features <- df_features[sample_index3, ] #subsetting the 80% of features
test3_features <- df_features[-sample_index3, ] #subsetting the 20% of features 
train3_labels <- df_labels[sample_index3] #attaching the labels that go with the 80%
test3_labels <- df_labels[-sample_index3] #attaching the labels that go with the 20%
```

Now the data is split into three seeds with a different 80/20 split in each. The data is ready for kNN modeling, where each split will be used to train and test the algorithm. 

## Part E

To identify the optimal value of k, I will begin by testing a wide range of k values using a loop, then narrow the range once a promising region is identified. The initial range will include k = 53, which is close to the square root of the number of observations and serves as a common starting point. My lowest value will be k = 3 rather than k = 1 to avoid overfitting, because with k = 1, the model classifies each observation based on a single neighbor, making it highly sensitive to outliers. All k values tested will be odd numbers, as kNN determines a class by voting among its nearest neighbors, and an even k risks a tie. Using a loop rather than individual crosstables allows for efficient comparison across many k values at once. Once the range is sufficiently narrowed, I will use crosstables to get a closer look at model performance. Accuracy will serve as the primary evaluation metric throughout the tuning process, with false rates examined once the options are narrowed down to a small number of possible k values.

### Testing: k=53

```{r echo=TRUE}
#Setting a wide range of k values
k_values <- c(3, 5, 15, 25, 35, 45, 53, 65, 75, 85, 95)

#Running the k algorithm 
